\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 5. Step 4 \'97 Rubrics & judge prompts (the heart of LLM-as-judge)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A 
\f0\b rubric
\f1\b0  defines what each score means so judging is 
\f0\b consistent
\f1\b0 , not vibes. In our harness each judge's 
\f2\i system prompt
\f1\i0  is a rubric. Properties we use:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Clear scale
\f1\b0  (0\'961) with anchor definitions.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Explicit edge cases
\f1\b0  (e.g. "if the answer says it's not in the KB and flags general knowledge, don't penalize").\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Structured output
